# Storage SQL discipline

Use Python's built-in `sqlite3` only — no ORM. Schemas are hand-written
`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` statements kept in `db.py`. All queries must be
parameterized with `?` placeholders — never string-format values into SQL.
Deduplication is enforced at the database layer via `UNIQUE` constraints plus
`INSERT OR IGNORE` (readings) or `ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE` (events), not by
check-then-insert in Python. Reads feeding the detection pipeline return
readings oldest-first (chronological); API read paths return most-recent-first.

**Rationale:** the schema is small enough that explicit SQL is clearer than an
ORM and showcases the dedup constraint directly; the ordering split matters
because detectors require chronological input while the API wants newest-first.
